WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.580
Good morning, Hank.
It's Tuesday.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.260
So, a Youtube employee once told me 
that almost all so-called low-engagement viewers

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:20.160
decide whether to watch a video in the first 12 seconds.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.800
And I don't want any low-engagement viewers today, hence the 12 seconds of silence.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:28.360
Also, how long is 12 seconds
of silence on YouTube? An eternity!

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:29.380
By the way, Hank, I'm in Amsterdam.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.940
This is not a green screen or anything.
It only looks impossibly beautiful.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.080
But anyway, the easiest and most obvious metric
for successful YouTube videos

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.840
is how many views it gets, and lately,
ours have been getting a lot of views.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.760
In the past month our new videos have averaged over 600,000 views.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.920
Meanwhile, a lot of other things are also
increasing the size of nerdfighteria.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.660
By the way, in a stunning turn of events,
it is raining in Amsterdam.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.200
So, I think I'm going to go inside.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.630
Rain's relationship with Amsterdam is like mine.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:56.340
It visited once and it was like "This is great,"
and then it kept coming back.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.360
This is better.
So nerdfighteria is growing in lots of ways.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.720
My books are reaching a much larger audience
than I ever imagined.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:05.400
I mean, Hank, I now regularly get emails
in all capital letters from 80-year-old grandmothers

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.820
that are like, "My granddaughter told me to read your book, and I did, and I liked it.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.720
And now my entire retirement home's book club is reading it.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.860
P.S. What happens to Anna's mom?"

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.040
And that's awesome because A,
we need more old people in nerdfighteria

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.900
and B, it's good to know that 80-year-olds
and teenagers ask the same questions.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:25.000
Also, Crash Course and SciShow and Mental Floss
all have large and growing audiences,

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.240
and the very way
that we find Youtube videos is changing.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.560
I mean, having a feed instead of a subscription box
means that we see more videos

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.680
from channels we aren't subscribed to 
and fewer from the ones we are subscribed to.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.740
So, here's my fear Hank: As communities grow,
especially if they grow without deepening,

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:43.020
they paradoxically often become less important.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.780
Like, a lot of people watch the television program,
"Two and a Half Men,"

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.740
but that show's community
is far less interesting and important

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.420
than a much less popular show, like "Supernatural."

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.580
Now, of course, bigger doesn't always
mean less important.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.180
The Harry Potter books, for instance, 
reached a fairly large audience.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.740
But still, that core community of Potter fans
who listened to Wizard Rock

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:04.720
and started the Harry Potter Alliance 
made big and lasting changes in the world,

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:05.880
and they're still making them.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.480
In short, Hank, if we're going to keep growing,
I want to make sure that we grow like that.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:12.900
So, Hank, we realized several years ago
that fame isn't very fulfilling.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:18.560
But what is tremendously fulfilling is the opportunity
to make stuff you like with people you like.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:24.560
So, I want to make that recommitment to nerdfighteria
that this is truly a collaboration among all of us.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.600
I think we should continue to make videos that explain stuff like health care costs and sovereign debt

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:32.540
and the war in Syria, and I also think we should
make videos about stuff that we care about

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.480
like the TFIOS movie, which I care about very much.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.780
But Hank, I also want to be making good and funny
and nerdy stuff with nerdfighteria.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.420
So, let's start today.
My last video was over four minutes long.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.540
Nerdfighters in Our Pants,
which sounds dirty, but isn't,

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.520
decided that it did not meet the definition
of an educational video,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.120
ergo, I am punished! 
By the way, for those of you

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.740
who are new, 
Our Pants is the forum.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.360
Our videos aren't allowed to be more
than four minutes long unless they're educational.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.040
Hank is my brother.
I like pizza.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.020
Hank likes corn dogs, and nerdfighters are nerds
who fight to make the world suck less.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:03.160
Also, Hank, according to the nerdfighters in Our Pants,
we both already owe punishments,

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.200
so we're both punished, 
and I'm doubly punished.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.100
So nerdfighters, we need lots 
of punishment suggestions in comments.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.580
Hank, thanks for the update video,
which might be even better than 12 seconds of silence

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.800
at getting non-nerdfighters to stop watching.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:17.540
I'm very excited for Emma Approved by the way, and I will see you on Friday.

